// An intrinsic has no AST body, so it contributes no call edge and no allocation fact — it is invisible
// to the transitivity walk. `string` is the one intrinsic family whose methods MINT heap: it is immutable,
// so every method that "changes" it returns a NEW owned string. Derived from the RETURN TYPE, so a string
// method added later is covered the day it is added, rather than from a list of names that would drift.
@noheap fn int32 tick(ref string a) { string c = a.trim(); return cast<int32>(c.length()); }
fn int32 main() { string x = " hi "; return tick(a: ref x); }
